    8     0.000   0.000   0.000   0.000    16.0          5.3     5.16
   12     0.000   0.000   0.000   0.000    16.0          7.1     5.32
   16     0.100   0.100   0.000   0.000    16.0          7.9     5.32

  K = 16 exposure: 10 realisations x 7.9 certifying turns x 116 pulsars = 9164 pulsar-certifications,
  with 1 SIGNAL certification(s) in total and 0 null certifications.
  Poisson 95 % upper bound on V25's null FPR: 0.0403 %

  ANSWER, PLAINLY: **STARVED ON SIGNAL, AND ITS NULL NEVER TESTED THE HIGH-
  COMB REGIME AT ALL.** Both halves matter and they are different failures:
    - STARVED on signal: 0.1 certifications per run is ONE event in the
      whole 10-realisation cell. Nothing about the channel's POWER can be
      read off it, and V25's 'every one of them on the correct fringe' is a
      statement about a single event.
    - The NULL arm's raw exposure looks large (~9164 pulsar-
      certifications) but it is a CHAIN: turn t certifies against t accumulated
      combs, capped at 8. With 7.9 certifying turns the comb count runs
      1, 2, 3, ... 8, so the exposure is spread across comb counts and only a
      small tail of it sits at 8, none at 16. V25 NEVER RAN A 16-COMB NULL.

  IS V25's ZERO EVEN IN TENSION WITH THIS RUN? Compare like with like -- the
  matched arm is C2 (synthetic array + uniform 10 pc priors), which IS V25's
  configuration:
    expected V25 null certs at the   C2 at 8 combs rate (0.022 %):   2.0   P(observe 0) = 0.139
    expected V25 null certs at the  C2 at 16 combs rate (0.065 %):   5.9   P(observe 0) = 0.003
  So V25's zero is CONSISTENT with this run's low-comb rate and says nothing
  about the high-comb rate. The premise 'V25 was clean at K = 16, V29 opened
  it' does not survive: V25 has no 16-comb null to be clean.

============================================================================================================
DELIVERABLE 12 -- THE MECHANISM: THE NULL-CALIBRATED FLOOR IS DEAD CODE
============================================================================================================
  The certification bar is  bar = max( ln K_i , floor_i + err_i )  where floor_i
  is the incoherent-comb floor recut at alpha = 0.05. Which term actually binds?

               arm   K   bar == lnK   med lnK   med floor  gap (nat)
      C0 real+real   2        99.7%      7.61        0.00      +7.61
      C0 real+real   8        98.9%      8.35        0.23      +8.11
      C0 real+real  16        96.0%      8.57        0.47      +8.10
   C1 real+uniform   2       100.0%      4.57        0.00      +4.57
   C1 real+uniform   8        98.9%      5.21        0.22      +4.99
   C1 real+uniform  16        94.7%      5.42        0.46      +4.96
  C2 synth+uniform   2       100.0%      4.55        0.00      +4.55
  C2 synth+uniform   8        99.1%      5.25        0.08      +5.18
  C2 synth+uniform  16        97.3%      5.44        0.21      +5.22

  ==> In EVERY arm and at EVERY comb count the trials term ln K_i exceeds the
      null-calibrated floor for 95-100 % of pulsars, by 5-8 nat. `recut_floor`
      and its alpha = 0.05 are therefore NEVER the operative gate in this
      regime: certification is decided by a pure Bonferroni-style trials bar
      whose realised false-alarm rate is NOT calibrated to anything. It is
      whatever the contrast statistic's tail happens to give, which depends on
      array geometry (C1 vs C2) and on comb count NON-monotonically.
      THAT is the shared defect, and it lives in vernier24.certify -- inherited
      unchanged by V25, V27, V28 and V29.

